00:00:03.090 --> 00:00:05.400
SARUI SUN: Welcome to
Best Practices for Privacy

00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:07.170
and Security in GCE.

00:00:07.170 --> 00:00:08.020
My name is Sarui.

00:00:08.020 --> 00:00:10.325
I'm a product manager on
the Compute Engine team.

00:00:10.325 --> 00:00:11.450
CACHE MCWHERTER: I'm Cache.

00:00:11.450 --> 00:00:14.370
I'm an IAM engineer, and I'm
ready for a 400 level little

00:00:14.370 --> 00:00:16.340
talk on the theory
of securitrons.

00:00:16.340 --> 00:00:17.840
SARUI SUN: Well,
Cache, you're going

00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:20.410
to have to wait just a little
bit because this is a 200 level

00:00:20.410 --> 00:00:20.910
talk.

00:00:20.910 --> 00:00:23.202
But we still have a lot of
great content for you today.

00:00:23.202 --> 00:00:24.790
So I wanted to get started.

00:00:24.790 --> 00:00:27.510
So over the past years,
we've learned a lot here

00:00:27.510 --> 00:00:30.900
at Google Cloud in securing
both our internal cloud

00:00:30.900 --> 00:00:32.580
and the clouds
for our customers.

00:00:32.580 --> 00:00:34.110
And the goal of
the next 50 minutes

00:00:34.110 --> 00:00:36.030
is for us to share
some of those learnings

00:00:36.030 --> 00:00:38.610
for you, to share some
of the best practices

00:00:38.610 --> 00:00:40.260
we've picked up so
that you don't have

00:00:40.260 --> 00:00:42.360
to make the same
mistakes that we did,

00:00:42.360 --> 00:00:45.390
and that you can take
off running when it

00:00:45.390 --> 00:00:47.400
comes to securing your cloud.

00:00:47.400 --> 00:00:49.015
So often when we
talk to customers,

00:00:49.015 --> 00:00:51.390
they'll ask us a question that
sound something like this.

00:00:51.390 --> 00:00:54.000
They'll say, hey, how do I
check off that security box?

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:57.960
How do I make sure my cloud is
completely, completely secure?

00:00:57.960 --> 00:01:00.180
And to that we say, well,
that's a good question.

00:01:00.180 --> 00:01:02.222
And Cache and I got in a
room we thought about it

00:01:02.222 --> 00:01:05.129
for a little bit, and it turns
out it's the craziest thing,

00:01:05.129 --> 00:01:07.560
you can actually
secure your cloud

00:01:07.560 --> 00:01:10.090
with just this one weird trick.

00:01:10.090 --> 00:01:11.560
It's pretty crazy.

00:01:11.560 --> 00:01:13.420
Are you ready for it?

00:01:13.420 --> 00:01:16.720
Just kidding,
that's not a thing.

00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:17.340
It'd be crazy.

00:01:17.340 --> 00:01:18.632
Wouldn't that be crazy, though?

00:01:18.632 --> 00:01:21.510
Like this whole talk would
be over in like two minutes.

00:01:21.510 --> 00:01:24.360
In reality what we
found in over the past

00:01:24.360 --> 00:01:26.700
years is that
security is a lot more

00:01:26.700 --> 00:01:29.700
of a dynamic contextual thing.

00:01:29.700 --> 00:01:31.380
So if you think
about it in terms

00:01:31.380 --> 00:01:34.740
of securing a physical space
like securing Moscone Center,

00:01:34.740 --> 00:01:38.040
the correct level of security
or the correct security posture

00:01:38.040 --> 00:01:41.472
for Moscone Center is going to
depend on a number of factors.

00:01:41.472 --> 00:01:43.305
It's going to depend
on what part of Moscone

00:01:43.305 --> 00:01:44.400
are you trying to secure?

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:46.192
Are you trying to secure
the front sidewalk

00:01:46.192 --> 00:01:48.510
or are you trying to
secure the generator room?

00:01:48.510 --> 00:01:50.010
It also is going
to depend on what's

00:01:50.010 --> 00:01:52.290
happening on Moscone Center?

00:01:52.290 --> 00:01:55.080
is this an open house
that anyone can attend,

00:01:55.080 --> 00:01:58.950
or there's the president in
town and giving a speech?

00:01:58.950 --> 00:02:01.380
And even more, if you are
in charge of the team that's

00:02:01.380 --> 00:02:05.190
securing Moscone Center is your
responsibility to just secure

00:02:05.190 --> 00:02:08.370
this building, or is it to
secure the entire complex,

00:02:08.370 --> 00:02:11.965
or is it to secure a nationwide
chain of convention centers?

00:02:11.965 --> 00:02:13.590
That's all going to
be a different part

00:02:13.590 --> 00:02:15.580
of the calculation
of the equation

00:02:15.580 --> 00:02:19.880
and it's going to change
how you approach security.

00:02:19.880 --> 00:02:23.150
And so when you look at
security posture, first of all,

00:02:23.150 --> 00:02:24.200
it lies on a spectrum.

00:02:24.200 --> 00:02:25.992
Regardless of what
you're trying to secure,

00:02:25.992 --> 00:02:28.280
it lies on a spectrum,
and we should recognize

00:02:28.280 --> 00:02:29.662
that it can be too loose.

00:02:29.662 --> 00:02:32.120
No matter what you're trying
to secure it can be too loose.

00:02:32.120 --> 00:02:33.720
It can be the Wild West.

00:02:33.720 --> 00:02:35.720
Anyone could do anything
and for sure bad things

00:02:35.720 --> 00:02:36.990
are going to happen there.

00:02:36.990 --> 00:02:38.690
But it can also be too strict.

00:02:38.690 --> 00:02:41.990
You can have too many rules,
too many things locked down.

00:02:41.990 --> 00:02:45.335
And that's bad too, because that
will lead to loss of agility.

00:02:45.335 --> 00:02:47.210
It might lead to an
antagonistic relationship

00:02:47.210 --> 00:02:49.220
between your security
teams and everyone else,

00:02:49.220 --> 00:02:51.290
and a number of other things.

00:02:51.290 --> 00:02:54.440
And so to start the talk I want
to set aside some principles

00:02:54.440 --> 00:02:57.350
to help you try to understand
and navigate the spectrum

00:02:57.350 --> 00:02:59.398
and get to the right spot there.

00:02:59.398 --> 00:03:01.190
So the first principle
I want to talk about

00:03:01.190 --> 00:03:02.870
is centralized administration.

00:03:02.870 --> 00:03:06.257
And this is the idea that if
you as a central security team--

00:03:06.257 --> 00:03:08.090
there should be a central
security team that

00:03:08.090 --> 00:03:12.148
has full awareness of
everything that's going on,

00:03:12.148 --> 00:03:14.690
or at least the ability to be
fully aware of what's going on,

00:03:14.690 --> 00:03:17.150
and also has responsibility
and governance

00:03:17.150 --> 00:03:19.820
and authority to govern over
everything that's going on.

00:03:19.820 --> 00:03:22.113
Centralized governance,
without that,

00:03:22.113 --> 00:03:24.530
if you don't have the ability
to know what all is going on

00:03:24.530 --> 00:03:26.720
or the authority to
enforce what's going on,

00:03:26.720 --> 00:03:29.250
you're almost
certainly too loose.

00:03:29.250 --> 00:03:31.550
But what we found is if
you just have centralized

00:03:31.550 --> 00:03:33.950
administration, you
may end up falling

00:03:33.950 --> 00:03:35.580
on the opposite end
of the spectrum,

00:03:35.580 --> 00:03:37.650
especially in a
larger organization.

00:03:37.650 --> 00:03:39.800
If you have a team of
hundreds or thousands

00:03:39.800 --> 00:03:42.170
and the responsibility for
securing and tracking down

00:03:42.170 --> 00:03:43.753
everything that's
happening is falling

00:03:43.753 --> 00:03:45.380
on the shoulders
of very few people,

00:03:45.380 --> 00:03:47.270
you may end up on the
opposite side of the spectrum

00:03:47.270 --> 00:03:48.890
where you're forced to
lock down everything

00:03:48.890 --> 00:03:50.240
or you're forced to
implement policies

00:03:50.240 --> 00:03:52.282
that you can't keep track
of because there's just

00:03:52.282 --> 00:03:53.090
so much going on.

00:03:53.090 --> 00:03:56.060
And so to that we introduce
a second principle

00:03:56.060 --> 00:03:56.828
called delegation.

00:03:56.828 --> 00:03:58.370
And this is the idea
that if you have

00:03:58.370 --> 00:04:00.578
a centralized administrative
team that they can still

00:04:00.578 --> 00:04:03.140
delegate administrative
tasks to a separate sub

00:04:03.140 --> 00:04:06.700
team or some other division
or some other set of folks,

00:04:06.700 --> 00:04:09.800
and that they can move much
quicker having delegated

00:04:09.800 --> 00:04:11.380
authority.

00:04:11.380 --> 00:04:13.630
And what we found is that
between those two principles

00:04:13.630 --> 00:04:15.790
you're able to get
closer to the center

00:04:15.790 --> 00:04:18.550
and get into what we call
the Goldilocks zone--

00:04:18.550 --> 00:04:20.680
It's a technical term.

00:04:20.680 --> 00:04:23.230
--where you basically
have the right mix

00:04:23.230 --> 00:04:26.410
of too loose and too strict.

00:04:26.410 --> 00:04:29.410
And notice what we've done
here is we've called it a zone.

00:04:29.410 --> 00:04:31.990
This is not a Goldilocks point,
and that's in recognition

00:04:31.990 --> 00:04:35.950
that the right place to
pick on the security posture

00:04:35.950 --> 00:04:38.560
is going to be very
contextually relevant.

00:04:38.560 --> 00:04:40.750
It's going to depend on
the context for your cloud

00:04:40.750 --> 00:04:44.925
just as it depended on the
context for Moscone Center.

00:04:44.925 --> 00:04:47.300
And so what we're going to do
within this presentation is

00:04:47.300 --> 00:04:49.820
we're going to start with the
building blocks for security,

00:04:49.820 --> 00:04:51.190
start with some of
the tools there,

00:04:51.190 --> 00:04:53.607
and we're going to build our
way up to some best practices

00:04:53.607 --> 00:04:54.710
that we've learned.

00:04:54.710 --> 00:04:57.740
But by doing so we'll allow
you to kind of pick up

00:04:57.740 --> 00:04:59.180
the best practices,
some of which

00:04:59.180 --> 00:05:01.670
you should do
regardless all the time,

00:05:01.670 --> 00:05:05.510
but the practice of some of
which will depend on what

00:05:05.510 --> 00:05:07.760
your security context is and
what's the right security

00:05:07.760 --> 00:05:10.690
posture for your organization.

00:05:10.690 --> 00:05:11.200
OK.

00:05:11.200 --> 00:05:13.480
So the first building
block, starting simple,

00:05:13.480 --> 00:05:15.100
are the identities.

00:05:15.100 --> 00:05:17.590
This is the set of
users and groups

00:05:17.590 --> 00:05:20.770
and services that you have
that Google Cloud knows about.

00:05:20.770 --> 00:05:23.290
Google Cloud knows about them
because Google Cloud Identity,

00:05:23.290 --> 00:05:27.435
which is our identity
service, knows about them.

00:05:27.435 --> 00:05:29.560
The quick thing I'll say
here is that we understand

00:05:29.560 --> 00:05:32.463
that some of you may have your
identities mastered elsewhere.

00:05:32.463 --> 00:05:33.880
In fact that might
be most of you.

00:05:33.880 --> 00:05:36.190
A lot of organizations
that we've talked to,

00:05:36.190 --> 00:05:38.880
they master their identities
in Active Directory.

00:05:38.880 --> 00:05:40.880
And I just want to call
out that it is possible.

00:05:40.880 --> 00:05:42.970
We do support you
syncing your identities

00:05:42.970 --> 00:05:45.352
from wherever you master them
into Google Cloud Identity

00:05:45.352 --> 00:05:46.810
so that you can
keep mastering them

00:05:46.810 --> 00:05:49.700
wherever they currently exist.

00:05:49.700 --> 00:05:51.473
Next, I want to talk
about resources.

00:05:51.473 --> 00:05:53.390
So these are the things
that you're ultimately

00:05:53.390 --> 00:05:54.417
trying to protect.

00:05:54.417 --> 00:05:56.000
When it comes to
Compute Engine, these

00:05:56.000 --> 00:05:58.670
might be your VMs and your
disks and your subnets

00:05:58.670 --> 00:06:01.320
and your images.

00:06:01.320 --> 00:06:05.540
But resources also apply to
any non Google Compute Engine

00:06:05.540 --> 00:06:07.940
resources inside GCP as well.

00:06:07.940 --> 00:06:11.180
Resources, of course, roll up
to one and only one project.

00:06:11.180 --> 00:06:14.310
These are our base
level of grouping.

00:06:14.310 --> 00:06:17.090
So all resources have
to belong to one project

00:06:17.090 --> 00:06:19.670
and projects also encapsulate
some other properties

00:06:19.670 --> 00:06:22.550
like the billing account,
quotas, permissions, and things

00:06:22.550 --> 00:06:25.210
like that.

00:06:25.210 --> 00:06:27.550
The next level up
is the organization.

00:06:27.550 --> 00:06:29.850
So this is an optional concept.

00:06:29.850 --> 00:06:31.533
You don't have to
have an organization,

00:06:31.533 --> 00:06:33.450
but we highly, highly
encourage it for reasons

00:06:33.450 --> 00:06:35.050
that I'll get into for a second.

00:06:35.050 --> 00:06:38.080
But these will act as the root
node for all GCP resources.

00:06:38.080 --> 00:06:40.260
So if you set up an
organization everything

00:06:40.260 --> 00:06:43.320
should roll up into
the organization.

00:06:43.320 --> 00:06:45.560
And finally, we have folders.

00:06:45.560 --> 00:06:48.380
These are an additional
optional grouping mechanism.

00:06:48.380 --> 00:06:50.450
They can contain other
projects or folders,

00:06:50.450 --> 00:06:53.690
and we found that this is a
great way to organize and make

00:06:53.690 --> 00:06:56.670
sense of your policies.

00:06:56.670 --> 00:06:59.300
So we're going to dive a little
bit more abstract in a sec,

00:06:59.300 --> 00:07:02.150
so bear with me,
but we'll then make

00:07:02.150 --> 00:07:05.060
it more concrete with some
examples and some best

00:07:05.060 --> 00:07:07.710
practices to put them together.

00:07:07.710 --> 00:07:09.830
So once you have the
resource hierarchy in place

00:07:09.830 --> 00:07:12.740
you can apply policies to
this resource hierarchy.

00:07:12.740 --> 00:07:16.040
Policies can be applied anywhere
in the resource hierarchy,

00:07:16.040 --> 00:07:17.570
and once they're
applied, a policy

00:07:17.570 --> 00:07:19.680
is then passed from the
parent to the child.

00:07:19.680 --> 00:07:22.820
So for example, if I set
a policy on department x

00:07:22.820 --> 00:07:25.760
it'll pass down to all the other
sub folders under department

00:07:25.760 --> 00:07:29.030
x as the folder that'll get
passed down into the projects

00:07:29.030 --> 00:07:31.790
and into the resources.

00:07:31.790 --> 00:07:33.892
There we go.

00:07:33.892 --> 00:07:35.225
There are two types of policies.

00:07:38.410 --> 00:07:40.160
The first one is
called IAM policies.

00:07:40.160 --> 00:07:42.850
So these are in the business
of granting permissions

00:07:42.850 --> 00:07:45.560
to particular identities
for particular resources.

00:07:45.560 --> 00:07:47.930
The second type is called
organization policies.

00:07:47.930 --> 00:07:50.770
These are in the business
of creating constraints.

00:07:50.770 --> 00:07:53.050
Let's talk about
IAM policies first.

00:07:53.050 --> 00:07:56.380
IAM policies control who is
allowed to do what on which

00:07:56.380 --> 00:07:57.370
resources.

00:07:57.370 --> 00:08:00.490
So it takes a set of identities,
like a user or a group

00:08:00.490 --> 00:08:03.910
or a service account, and then
it takes a set of resources,

00:08:03.910 --> 00:08:07.390
like a folder or a project
or individual resources,

00:08:07.390 --> 00:08:11.080
and it says these identities
will have these permissions

00:08:11.080 --> 00:08:12.790
on these resources.

00:08:12.790 --> 00:08:16.250
And it does that through
a particular role.

00:08:16.250 --> 00:08:17.700
So in this particular
example, we

00:08:17.700 --> 00:08:20.920
might give, for example, Alice
the compute instance admin

00:08:20.920 --> 00:08:23.440
role on an individual VM.

00:08:23.440 --> 00:08:26.080
And what that'll do is it'll
give Alice permissions to do

00:08:26.080 --> 00:08:29.640
instance administrative actions
on that VM, like creating VMs

00:08:29.640 --> 00:08:31.510
or like creating it,
what's already created,

00:08:31.510 --> 00:08:35.140
deleting it, starting
it or stopping it.

00:08:35.140 --> 00:08:36.659
So what I find is
that it helps make

00:08:36.659 --> 00:08:37.679
things a little more concrete.

00:08:37.679 --> 00:08:39.600
If we show what this
looks like in the UI,

00:08:39.600 --> 00:08:42.240
you can of course set
permissions in G Cloud

00:08:42.240 --> 00:08:43.250
and the APIs as well.

00:08:43.250 --> 00:08:45.000
But for the purposes
of this presentation,

00:08:45.000 --> 00:08:46.300
we'll go in the UI.

00:08:46.300 --> 00:08:49.170
So here I am in the instances
list in Compute Engine.

00:08:49.170 --> 00:08:50.700
I can select a particular VM.

00:08:50.700 --> 00:08:54.480
Here I've selected instance
1,000, and now that I specified

00:08:54.480 --> 00:08:56.160
the resource I just
need to say who

00:08:56.160 --> 00:08:58.090
can do what on that resource.

00:08:58.090 --> 00:08:59.790
And so if I click the
Add Members button

00:08:59.790 --> 00:09:03.040
over in the top right there, I
can answer those two questions.

00:09:03.040 --> 00:09:05.700
So it'll ask me who do you
want to have this policy,

00:09:05.700 --> 00:09:10.240
and also what role do you
want to apply to this user?

00:09:10.240 --> 00:09:11.850
So in this particular
case, I'm going

00:09:11.850 --> 00:09:13.655
to give Alice this
particular role,

00:09:13.655 --> 00:09:16.155
and the role I'm going to give
is the instance administrator

00:09:16.155 --> 00:09:16.807
role.

00:09:16.807 --> 00:09:19.140
So that's just walking through
what the previous example

00:09:19.140 --> 00:09:22.420
concretely looked like.

00:09:22.420 --> 00:09:24.500
Next, the second type
of policy is called

00:09:24.500 --> 00:09:26.280
the organizational policy.

00:09:26.280 --> 00:09:28.940
So these are in the business
of placing restrictions

00:09:28.940 --> 00:09:30.470
on your resources.

00:09:30.470 --> 00:09:32.540
And some examples
of that are you

00:09:32.540 --> 00:09:35.420
might want to restrict
the specific types of APIs

00:09:35.420 --> 00:09:37.700
or a specific set
of services that

00:09:37.700 --> 00:09:41.120
can be used in a particular
folder or a project.

00:09:41.120 --> 00:09:44.325
You might want to restrict a set
of VMs that have external IPs,

00:09:44.325 --> 00:09:45.950
or the third example
there is you might

00:09:45.950 --> 00:09:47.450
want to restrict a
set of users that

00:09:47.450 --> 00:09:50.690
can be added to IAM policies.

00:09:50.690 --> 00:09:52.520
Concretely what
this looks like is

00:09:52.520 --> 00:09:56.480
if you go into a project or
a folder or an organization

00:09:56.480 --> 00:09:59.270
and you go into the IAM
section and then the org policy

00:09:59.270 --> 00:10:02.660
subsection, you can see the full
list of organizational policies

00:10:02.660 --> 00:10:03.860
that we support.

00:10:03.860 --> 00:10:07.640
And the important thing here
is we offer a lot of them.

00:10:07.640 --> 00:10:09.633
We're adding to that
list all the time.

00:10:09.633 --> 00:10:12.050
And so one thing we recommend
is just when you're starting

00:10:12.050 --> 00:10:13.820
your Clouds appointment
or even if you've already

00:10:13.820 --> 00:10:16.010
got going, if you haven't
looked through all of these

00:10:16.010 --> 00:10:16.640
just take a look.

00:10:16.640 --> 00:10:18.932
It's kind of like flipping
through your iPhone settings

00:10:18.932 --> 00:10:21.590
when you first set it up.

00:10:21.590 --> 00:10:23.640
Here's a concrete
example of that.

00:10:23.640 --> 00:10:27.570
So suppose I'm an administrator
and I want to do my job well,

00:10:27.570 --> 00:10:31.010
and part of that is
preventing data exfiltration.

00:10:31.010 --> 00:10:32.930
So one org policy
that we find is

00:10:32.930 --> 00:10:36.050
very effective in helping to do
that is the domain restricted

00:10:36.050 --> 00:10:37.370
sharing org policy.

00:10:37.370 --> 00:10:40.980
So what I can use this for is I
can dictate that my developers

00:10:40.980 --> 00:10:42.800
they're not able
to share resources

00:10:42.800 --> 00:10:44.810
to users outside
of my organization

00:10:44.810 --> 00:10:48.860
unless I give them
explicit access to do so.

00:10:48.860 --> 00:10:52.400
Similarly, another vector
for data exfiltration

00:10:52.400 --> 00:10:55.490
might be external
attacks that go and look

00:10:55.490 --> 00:10:59.060
for VMs in my organization
with external IP addresses.

00:10:59.060 --> 00:11:01.970
And so another organization
policy that I can set

00:11:01.970 --> 00:11:08.600
is one that restricts
the set of VM instances

00:11:08.600 --> 00:11:10.850
in my organization with
external IP addresses.

00:11:10.850 --> 00:11:14.550
And so I will have knowledge
about all of the VMs

00:11:14.550 --> 00:11:17.970
that are in my organization
that have external IPs.

00:11:17.970 --> 00:11:18.470
OK.

00:11:18.470 --> 00:11:20.240
So let's put this all together.

00:11:20.240 --> 00:11:21.710
We're back to the hierarchy.

00:11:21.710 --> 00:11:23.690
Resources inherit the
policies that we just

00:11:23.690 --> 00:11:25.783
talked about from their parent.

00:11:25.783 --> 00:11:27.200
And so we can see
how we can start

00:11:27.200 --> 00:11:29.580
to apply some of the principles
that we talked about before.

00:11:29.580 --> 00:11:31.122
So the first principle
I talked about

00:11:31.122 --> 00:11:32.570
was centralized administration.

00:11:32.570 --> 00:11:35.450
You need to give away for some
central administrative team

00:11:35.450 --> 00:11:37.410
to understand what
all is going on

00:11:37.410 --> 00:11:39.572
and to have some broad
authority over that.

00:11:39.572 --> 00:11:41.030
So what you can
do, for example, is

00:11:41.030 --> 00:11:44.690
you can have an IAM policy
which gives the compute viewer

00:11:44.690 --> 00:11:46.490
role-- this is view
only access to compute

00:11:46.490 --> 00:11:50.060
resources. --to your
organizational admin group,

00:11:50.060 --> 00:11:52.490
and then once you apply that
policy to the organization

00:11:52.490 --> 00:11:56.330
node you'll see that it
flows down to all the folders

00:11:56.330 --> 00:12:00.210
and projects and resources
within that node automatically.

00:12:00.210 --> 00:12:02.780
And this will apply to
all the present resources

00:12:02.780 --> 00:12:04.280
in your organization,
but it'll also

00:12:04.280 --> 00:12:06.530
apply to all the future
resources in your organization

00:12:06.530 --> 00:12:07.073
as well.

00:12:07.073 --> 00:12:08.990
And so you can start to
see how this gives you

00:12:08.990 --> 00:12:12.340
a bit of an easier time in
doing centralized administration

00:12:12.340 --> 00:12:14.840
because you can imagine if you
didn't have this organization

00:12:14.840 --> 00:12:16.790
node, you might be
chasing after thousands

00:12:16.790 --> 00:12:20.600
of projects that get spun
up in your organization.

00:12:20.600 --> 00:12:23.460
Secondly, we can achieve the
second principle as well,

00:12:23.460 --> 00:12:24.840
which is that of delegation.

00:12:24.840 --> 00:12:27.920
So if you imagine you
have team A over here.

00:12:27.920 --> 00:12:32.300
Team A has an SRE team and as
a centralized administrative

00:12:32.300 --> 00:12:35.900
group you want to delegate
instance administration actions

00:12:35.900 --> 00:12:41.090
to that particular team so you
can set the policy on team A's

00:12:41.090 --> 00:12:45.915
folder to give instance
admin role to the entire team

00:12:45.915 --> 00:12:48.860
A SRE group, and that
will also propagate down

00:12:48.860 --> 00:12:51.490
to all the projects and
resources under team A.

00:12:51.490 --> 00:12:55.400
And so now you've effectively
delegated permissions

00:12:55.400 --> 00:12:59.960
for that particular team
for that particular group.

00:12:59.960 --> 00:13:02.330
The same thing applies
with org policies.

00:13:02.330 --> 00:13:05.000
So suppose I have a
folder and in that

00:13:05.000 --> 00:13:07.700
folder is supposed to
categorize a set of workloads

00:13:07.700 --> 00:13:08.760
that are internal only.

00:13:08.760 --> 00:13:10.260
I don't want these
workloads to ever

00:13:10.260 --> 00:13:13.310
have an external IP address,
which I'm showing here

00:13:13.310 --> 00:13:14.630
as the internal folder.

00:13:14.630 --> 00:13:18.090
I can set a org policy on
that particular folder,

00:13:18.090 --> 00:13:21.810
which restricts all VMs
with external IP addresses.

00:13:21.810 --> 00:13:25.190
And in doing so I'll find that
the org policy automatically

00:13:25.190 --> 00:13:28.160
flows down to all the
resources and projects

00:13:28.160 --> 00:13:29.600
in that particular folder.

00:13:29.600 --> 00:13:31.910
And so here again I'll
have a much easier time

00:13:31.910 --> 00:13:35.070
of governing my organization.

00:13:35.070 --> 00:13:35.710
OK.

00:13:35.710 --> 00:13:37.010
So that was some of
the building blocks.

00:13:37.010 --> 00:13:38.380
I'm going to turn
it over to Cache,

00:13:38.380 --> 00:13:40.630
who's going to start building
them up into best practices.

00:13:40.630 --> 00:13:41.630
CACHE MCWHERTER: Thanks.

00:13:45.150 --> 00:13:48.960
Now, as you might expect we
talk to a lot of customers.

00:13:48.960 --> 00:13:50.807
Day in and day out
this is all we do.

00:13:50.807 --> 00:13:53.140
We try to help them understand
where they're coming from

00:13:53.140 --> 00:13:55.515
and how to apply the security
tools that we're developing

00:13:55.515 --> 00:13:58.260
to best solve their
security problems

00:13:58.260 --> 00:14:00.690
and manage their cloud.

00:14:00.690 --> 00:14:02.850
And one of the common
problems that we have and we

00:14:02.850 --> 00:14:04.770
find that customers
have is that they

00:14:04.770 --> 00:14:09.310
don't know how to
most effectively apply

00:14:09.310 --> 00:14:12.960
the resource
hierarchy to organize

00:14:12.960 --> 00:14:15.630
their resources in our cloud.

00:14:15.630 --> 00:14:18.630
We find that customers will
sometimes not really know

00:14:18.630 --> 00:14:21.930
what to do and they'll end up
with the sprawl of hundreds

00:14:21.930 --> 00:14:24.210
or thousands of
projects, all of which

00:14:24.210 --> 00:14:26.760
have different
policies and settings

00:14:26.760 --> 00:14:29.370
and so forth where
they have to manage

00:14:29.370 --> 00:14:30.780
each of these individually.

00:14:30.780 --> 00:14:34.410
Likewise, other companies think
that maybe the best thing to do

00:14:34.410 --> 00:14:37.500
is to take their org chart and
sort of push it into the folder

00:14:37.500 --> 00:14:39.240
structure and use that.

00:14:39.240 --> 00:14:41.280
We find that the best
hierarchies don't

00:14:41.280 --> 00:14:45.080
do either of those, and
instead they sort of

00:14:45.080 --> 00:14:46.980
build off of two concepts.

00:14:46.980 --> 00:14:49.850
The first concept is delegating
authority as Sarui mentioned,

00:14:49.850 --> 00:14:52.190
and the second is grouping
resources of like risk

00:14:52.190 --> 00:14:55.700
together where you can apply
similar security policies

00:14:55.700 --> 00:14:58.830
to the same sets of resources.

00:14:58.830 --> 00:15:00.600
To show you what that
looks like here's

00:15:00.600 --> 00:15:02.250
a simple organization here.

00:15:02.250 --> 00:15:05.820
A simple company that's decided
to build a couple applications

00:15:05.820 --> 00:15:09.030
inside of Google
Cloud, app1 and app2.

00:15:09.030 --> 00:15:13.320
In order to provide delegated
authority they created folders

00:15:13.320 --> 00:15:15.480
to represent each of
their applications

00:15:15.480 --> 00:15:17.183
and underneath
the applications--

00:15:17.183 --> 00:15:19.350
and this is a representation
of delegating authority

00:15:19.350 --> 00:15:21.150
to those teams. --they
granted the service

00:15:21.150 --> 00:15:26.070
teams that manage app1 and
app2 access to those photos.

00:15:26.070 --> 00:15:28.170
Below the folders there
are separate projects,

00:15:28.170 --> 00:15:31.710
each of them indicating whether
the resources within are

00:15:31.710 --> 00:15:33.870
pre-production development
environment resources

00:15:33.870 --> 00:15:37.530
or whether they're production
resources with critical data.

00:15:37.530 --> 00:15:40.920
And then this is
a sense grouping

00:15:40.920 --> 00:15:43.710
resources in accordance to their
security profile and security

00:15:43.710 --> 00:15:44.340
risk.

00:15:44.340 --> 00:15:47.513
The pre-fraud environments don't
need to be lockdown super hard.

00:15:47.513 --> 00:15:48.930
All the developers
can have access

00:15:48.930 --> 00:15:51.777
to deploy code,
access data, whatever

00:15:51.777 --> 00:15:53.610
they need to debug and
develop their system.

00:15:53.610 --> 00:15:55.443
The production environment
on the other hand

00:15:55.443 --> 00:15:57.867
is going to be locked
down much more tightly.

00:15:57.867 --> 00:15:59.700
Only the on-callers are
going to have access

00:15:59.700 --> 00:16:03.000
to fiddle with these projects
and their production systems

00:16:03.000 --> 00:16:05.400
are going to be responsible
for pushing coding

00:16:05.400 --> 00:16:09.040
and data to these environments.

00:16:09.040 --> 00:16:10.950
Once an application
scale is large enough

00:16:10.950 --> 00:16:14.148
you can imagine continuing
to apply the hierarchy

00:16:14.148 --> 00:16:16.440
to break down the application
and manage the complexity

00:16:16.440 --> 00:16:17.640
they're in.

00:16:17.640 --> 00:16:20.820
Here, for instance,
the application two

00:16:20.820 --> 00:16:23.370
has broken itself into two
sub-components, a front end

00:16:23.370 --> 00:16:26.880
and a back end, for instance,
and the administrators

00:16:26.880 --> 00:16:30.090
of application two were
responsible or delegating

00:16:30.090 --> 00:16:32.340
authority to the front end
team and the back end team

00:16:32.340 --> 00:16:34.320
to manage those
projects independently.

00:16:34.320 --> 00:16:36.330
And they'll set those
things up without having

00:16:36.330 --> 00:16:37.705
to come back to
the administrator

00:16:37.705 --> 00:16:38.820
to have to configure it.

00:16:41.710 --> 00:16:43.990
Now, to look at a more
complicated example using

00:16:43.990 --> 00:16:46.150
both IAM policy and
organization policy,

00:16:46.150 --> 00:16:48.380
you can do some
pretty cool things.

00:16:48.380 --> 00:16:52.230
So here we have a
large organization,

00:16:52.230 --> 00:16:53.180
fairly complicated.

00:16:53.180 --> 00:16:55.710
There're numerous teams,
numerous organization,

00:16:55.710 --> 00:16:58.830
numerous applications
within their hundreds

00:16:58.830 --> 00:17:02.030
of projects,
potentially thousands.

00:17:02.030 --> 00:17:04.605
As a security administrator
this makes me nervous.

00:17:04.605 --> 00:17:06.230
They're all deploying
virtual machines.

00:17:06.230 --> 00:17:07.813
I have no idea what
they're deploying.

00:17:07.813 --> 00:17:10.069
I want to make sure that
my company is making sure

00:17:10.069 --> 00:17:12.680
that the only things that
are running inside those VMs

00:17:12.680 --> 00:17:13.880
are trusted code.

00:17:13.880 --> 00:17:16.113
I need to put down an
operating system standard.

00:17:16.113 --> 00:17:18.530
I need to make sure that it's
running all the daemons, all

00:17:18.530 --> 00:17:21.722
the logging, all the security
protection infrastructure

00:17:21.722 --> 00:17:23.430
that I need into those
operating systems.

00:17:23.430 --> 00:17:25.369
I need to make sure
that all the developers

00:17:25.369 --> 00:17:28.290
inside my organization
are using those images.

00:17:28.290 --> 00:17:30.980
So what I'm going to do is I'm
going to hire an image team.

00:17:30.980 --> 00:17:32.330
I probably have one already.

00:17:32.330 --> 00:17:34.973
I'm going to pull off a
section of my policy hierarchy

00:17:34.973 --> 00:17:36.890
and I'm going to grant
access to my image team

00:17:36.890 --> 00:17:39.600
to manage those images.

00:17:39.600 --> 00:17:42.650
They're going to create some
images, test them, vet them,

00:17:42.650 --> 00:17:44.900
and then they're going to
publish them to this project

00:17:44.900 --> 00:17:48.480
and share those resources with
the rest of my organization.

00:17:48.480 --> 00:17:51.140
Now I can apply an
organizational policy called

00:17:51.140 --> 00:17:53.840
Trusted Image Projects to
the rest of my organization

00:17:53.840 --> 00:17:56.870
to lockdown and make sure that
the only operating systems

00:17:56.870 --> 00:18:00.320
that only boot disks that
can be used in my compute VMs

00:18:00.320 --> 00:18:04.040
are created and pushed by
the Golden Images Project.

00:18:04.040 --> 00:18:06.895
And this allows me to have faith
and confidence in the software

00:18:06.895 --> 00:18:08.020
that's running in my cloud.

00:18:10.660 --> 00:18:11.610
I'll wait for a photo.

00:18:15.830 --> 00:18:18.350
So now that we have
a policy hierarchy,

00:18:18.350 --> 00:18:21.630
the next big question that
a lot of customers have is,

00:18:21.630 --> 00:18:23.090
how do I figure
out how to assign

00:18:23.090 --> 00:18:26.810
the roles to my employees and
my services, and grant access?

00:18:26.810 --> 00:18:28.160
Now, this is a hard problem.

00:18:28.160 --> 00:18:30.800
This is fundamentally
the most difficult part

00:18:30.800 --> 00:18:34.910
of managing access control
and security in our system.

00:18:34.910 --> 00:18:36.620
You need to essentially
have someone

00:18:36.620 --> 00:18:38.810
who's capable of making
sense of the system

00:18:38.810 --> 00:18:41.330
and granting access that's
appropriate for business

00:18:41.330 --> 00:18:44.960
requirement to the
service or the user that's

00:18:44.960 --> 00:18:46.640
getting such access.

00:18:46.640 --> 00:18:48.950
I can't just make
that problem go away.

00:18:48.950 --> 00:18:50.335
I'm sorry.

00:18:50.335 --> 00:18:51.710
But we can help
you a little bit.

00:18:51.710 --> 00:18:56.570
We can help you build a set of
standards for deploying access

00:18:56.570 --> 00:18:58.970
grants that make sense for you.

00:18:58.970 --> 00:19:01.640
So as you come to
Google Cloud, you've

00:19:01.640 --> 00:19:04.400
probably encountered what we
call the primitive roles called

00:19:04.400 --> 00:19:05.690
owner, editor, and viewer.

00:19:05.690 --> 00:19:07.730
And these come out of the box.

00:19:07.730 --> 00:19:11.090
They're extremely
broad grants of access.

00:19:11.090 --> 00:19:12.980
We don't recommend
using them for anything.

00:19:12.980 --> 00:19:14.140
It's a production system.

00:19:14.140 --> 00:19:15.710
They're only there
to get you started

00:19:15.710 --> 00:19:19.378
and to help you kick
the tires essentially.

00:19:19.378 --> 00:19:21.170
And the reason that
they're not recommended

00:19:21.170 --> 00:19:22.587
is because they
don't satisfy what

00:19:22.587 --> 00:19:24.930
we call the principle
of least privilege,

00:19:24.930 --> 00:19:27.920
wherein you grant
an access that's

00:19:27.920 --> 00:19:30.605
commiserate with the minimum
requirements for each employee

00:19:30.605 --> 00:19:31.730
and service in your system.

00:19:34.550 --> 00:19:36.380
Now, when you're
designing roles it's

00:19:36.380 --> 00:19:40.520
important to remember again that
you don't want to specifically

00:19:40.520 --> 00:19:42.980
identify every single permission
and every single object

00:19:42.980 --> 00:19:45.650
that the employee is
going to have to access.

00:19:45.650 --> 00:19:48.320
In general we find there's
like a Goldilocks zone

00:19:48.320 --> 00:19:52.280
where you are going to grant
reasonable level access that

00:19:52.280 --> 00:19:55.040
contains all of the sets
of things that are in line

00:19:55.040 --> 00:19:56.960
with the business
requirements of the user,

00:19:56.960 --> 00:19:59.715
of the engineer or the
service that you're deploying.

00:19:59.715 --> 00:20:01.340
You don't want to be
too strict and you

00:20:01.340 --> 00:20:02.423
don't want to be to loose.

00:20:04.890 --> 00:20:07.050
Now, nine out of 10
security engineers

00:20:07.050 --> 00:20:11.080
love our pre-defined roles
to achieve this purpose.

00:20:11.080 --> 00:20:12.960
They're designed
by Google engineers

00:20:12.960 --> 00:20:15.390
and PNs based on
feedback we've gotten

00:20:15.390 --> 00:20:17.210
from all of our customers.

00:20:17.210 --> 00:20:20.750
They're representative the
kinds of roles in segregations

00:20:20.750 --> 00:20:26.340
of access and responsibility
that our customers

00:20:26.340 --> 00:20:27.420
have been asking for.

00:20:27.420 --> 00:20:29.730
They've been applying
to their own resources.

00:20:29.730 --> 00:20:32.100
And so, for instance,
here you can

00:20:32.100 --> 00:20:34.590
see compute has provided
you with a number of roles

00:20:34.590 --> 00:20:37.740
such as instance admin, network
admin, and load balancer admin,

00:20:37.740 --> 00:20:40.440
because these are roles
that are naturally

00:20:40.440 --> 00:20:43.230
managed by separate
individuals in a large number

00:20:43.230 --> 00:20:44.497
of organizations.

00:20:44.497 --> 00:20:46.830
The network administrator is
responsible for making sure

00:20:46.830 --> 00:20:49.390
that the firewalls are locked
down, and so forth and so on.

00:20:49.390 --> 00:20:51.810
Whereas the instance admin is
responsible for making sure

00:20:51.810 --> 00:20:56.230
that the Hadoop cluster is
up and running, for instance.

00:20:56.230 --> 00:20:57.720
Now, that final
security engineer

00:20:57.720 --> 00:21:01.020
who wasn't happy enough
with the predefined roles

00:21:01.020 --> 00:21:04.650
is usually satisfied and made
happy with our custom roles.

00:21:04.650 --> 00:21:07.050
They're used in
special circumstances

00:21:07.050 --> 00:21:09.330
where the predefined roles
don't line up exactly

00:21:09.330 --> 00:21:11.050
with your business requirements.

00:21:11.050 --> 00:21:13.652
For instance, we find
that a lot of customers

00:21:13.652 --> 00:21:15.360
will combine a number
of predefined roles

00:21:15.360 --> 00:21:18.420
into a single
role, a custom role

00:21:18.420 --> 00:21:20.040
so that they can
grant it more easily

00:21:20.040 --> 00:21:21.870
and make sure that
all sets of accesses

00:21:21.870 --> 00:21:26.820
are present on any resource
which they grant this access.

00:21:26.820 --> 00:21:29.910
Another common pattern is
that a security engineer sees

00:21:29.910 --> 00:21:33.660
a predefined role that exactly
matches their requirements,

00:21:33.660 --> 00:21:35.850
but there's that
one weird permission

00:21:35.850 --> 00:21:37.330
inside that they
really don't want,

00:21:37.330 --> 00:21:41.670
so they want to take that
away from their engineers.

00:21:41.670 --> 00:21:45.630
Now, when you're granting
access and assigning

00:21:45.630 --> 00:21:47.910
roles to your
employees and services

00:21:47.910 --> 00:21:51.600
it's quite easy to figure out
when you've gotten it wrong

00:21:51.600 --> 00:21:54.900
and you've under provisioned
the access to your resources.

00:21:54.900 --> 00:21:56.490
The engineers and
the service are

00:21:56.490 --> 00:21:58.810
going to complain in
one way or another,

00:21:58.810 --> 00:22:00.720
and you're going to get
a ticket or something

00:22:00.720 --> 00:22:06.517
to fix the assignment of
authority, fix the grants.

00:22:06.517 --> 00:22:08.850
But if you've over provisioned
you don't get any notice.

00:22:08.850 --> 00:22:11.340
No one comes along and tells
you I have too much power.

00:22:11.340 --> 00:22:12.020
I'm so sorry.

00:22:12.020 --> 00:22:15.180
Could you take some away?

00:22:15.180 --> 00:22:17.610
I'm happy to let you know
that we're rolling out

00:22:17.610 --> 00:22:21.250
tools that are going to help you
adjust these kinds of problems.

00:22:21.250 --> 00:22:24.600
One is, for instance, called
the IAM role recommender which

00:22:24.600 --> 00:22:26.940
looks at the access patterns
of users and services

00:22:26.940 --> 00:22:32.280
in your system, in your cloud,
and based on historical traffic

00:22:32.280 --> 00:22:36.120
gives you a future prediction
of what kinds of roles

00:22:36.120 --> 00:22:38.740
you might want to have
instead given to the user.

00:22:38.740 --> 00:22:41.730
And this allows you to make
more fine grain access control

00:22:41.730 --> 00:22:44.970
and apply the principle of
these privilege in your cloud

00:22:44.970 --> 00:22:47.730
at scale.

00:22:47.730 --> 00:22:50.705
So now another kind of
problem that we see--

00:22:50.705 --> 00:22:51.830
and it happens to everyone.

00:22:51.830 --> 00:22:52.670
It happens to us.

00:22:52.670 --> 00:22:53.700
It happens to me.

00:22:53.700 --> 00:22:55.760
It happens to our customers.

00:22:55.760 --> 00:22:58.700
--they often will get into a
situation in which they'll find

00:22:58.700 --> 00:23:02.060
a policy that looks like this.

00:23:02.060 --> 00:23:05.630
And it's hard to make sense
of why this policy looks

00:23:05.630 --> 00:23:07.850
the way it looks.

00:23:07.850 --> 00:23:11.120
Here you can see a number of
engineers have different levels

00:23:11.120 --> 00:23:13.580
of access to this project.

00:23:13.580 --> 00:23:16.400
I have no idea why any
of these roles exist.

00:23:16.400 --> 00:23:18.770
Some companies have
constructed spreadsheets

00:23:18.770 --> 00:23:21.020
to try to keep track of
the historical reasons

00:23:21.020 --> 00:23:23.360
about why every one of these
grants have been created.

00:23:23.360 --> 00:23:24.650
That's one strategy.

00:23:24.650 --> 00:23:26.390
The strategy we
recommend instead

00:23:26.390 --> 00:23:28.520
is to use what we
call a security

00:23:28.520 --> 00:23:34.550
group based model which sort
of models the Rbac philosophy.

00:23:34.550 --> 00:23:37.070
Instead of granting
roles to users

00:23:37.070 --> 00:23:39.890
individually in an ad hoc
way, what we recommend

00:23:39.890 --> 00:23:41.890
is you create security
groups that represent

00:23:41.890 --> 00:23:44.180
the conceptual roles
and responsibilities

00:23:44.180 --> 00:23:46.875
each engineer might
have to a resource.

00:23:46.875 --> 00:23:48.500
Here, for instance,
team grilled cheese

00:23:48.500 --> 00:23:51.230
has been broken down into
data scientists, debuggers,

00:23:51.230 --> 00:23:53.150
and on-callers.

00:23:53.150 --> 00:23:57.290
Now I can grant access to that
project to each of these roles

00:23:57.290 --> 00:23:59.735
and responsibilities
based on what

00:23:59.735 --> 00:24:00.860
they should have access to.

00:24:00.860 --> 00:24:04.180
Data scientists get to run
through the prediction engine,

00:24:04.180 --> 00:24:06.020
debuggers get to
run some queries

00:24:06.020 --> 00:24:09.380
and access some other
data, and on-callers

00:24:09.380 --> 00:24:11.080
get to do everything they want.

00:24:11.080 --> 00:24:13.120
Yeah.

00:24:13.120 --> 00:24:14.800
And then I can
independently manage

00:24:14.800 --> 00:24:17.092
the sets of users
and services that

00:24:17.092 --> 00:24:18.550
are members of the
security groups,

00:24:18.550 --> 00:24:22.540
and then I can audit each of
these categories independently.

00:24:22.540 --> 00:24:25.150
It's often usually much easier
to understand whether Cache

00:24:25.150 --> 00:24:29.020
belongs to team grilled cheese
than it is to decide whether he

00:24:29.020 --> 00:24:31.510
should have AutoML predictor
on some random project

00:24:31.510 --> 00:24:34.963
in your organization.

00:24:34.963 --> 00:24:36.880
Before I go on from role
construction and role

00:24:36.880 --> 00:24:39.220
assignment I'd like to talk
about one other pattern

00:24:39.220 --> 00:24:44.150
that we've been seeing
a lot of benefits from.

00:24:44.150 --> 00:24:45.890
That's break glass success.

00:24:45.890 --> 00:24:47.560
So one of the
common failure modes

00:24:47.560 --> 00:24:50.830
that organizations
both inside of Google

00:24:50.830 --> 00:24:53.590
and outside of Google
encounter with Google Cloud

00:24:53.590 --> 00:24:56.560
is that senior engineers
and on-call engineers

00:24:56.560 --> 00:24:58.600
end up getting
lots of privileges

00:24:58.600 --> 00:25:00.310
on production resources.

00:25:00.310 --> 00:25:03.340
Often they're an owner or often
they have administrator rights

00:25:03.340 --> 00:25:06.880
and they carry this as what
we call ambient authority.

00:25:06.880 --> 00:25:10.240
What this means is that it's
quite easy for an on caller

00:25:10.240 --> 00:25:13.450
to accidentally visit the
wrong project, for instance,

00:25:13.450 --> 00:25:15.730
and do something destructive
to that project thinking

00:25:15.730 --> 00:25:18.710
they might have been
working on another project.

00:25:18.710 --> 00:25:20.860
This is not a great
situation, especially when

00:25:20.860 --> 00:25:23.920
your business is on the line.

00:25:23.920 --> 00:25:26.080
What we recommend instead
is creating some sort

00:25:26.080 --> 00:25:29.860
of break glass pattern
for this kind of access

00:25:29.860 --> 00:25:31.960
for privileged access.

00:25:31.960 --> 00:25:37.180
One way you can do this is
with predefined IAM roles

00:25:37.180 --> 00:25:39.550
such as a project IAM Admin.

00:25:39.550 --> 00:25:41.930
Project IAM Admin does
not give you the ability

00:25:41.930 --> 00:25:44.980
to do anything on a project
except for set policy

00:25:44.980 --> 00:25:46.100
and change the policy.

00:25:46.100 --> 00:25:49.720
So instead of wandering
over to the Cloud console

00:25:49.720 --> 00:25:54.370
and deleting some VMs, they have
to contemplatively first grant

00:25:54.370 --> 00:25:59.870
themselves access to delete a
VM and then go and delete a VM.

00:25:59.870 --> 00:26:02.390
We find that the break
glass pattern is actually

00:26:02.390 --> 00:26:04.130
enhanced and made
a lot more secure

00:26:04.130 --> 00:26:06.110
by application of automation.

00:26:06.110 --> 00:26:09.440
And you can use automation such
as Jenkins, Terraform, Cloud

00:26:09.440 --> 00:26:11.930
Functions, anything
that you have in house

00:26:11.930 --> 00:26:15.890
to facilitate both the
breaking of the glass,

00:26:15.890 --> 00:26:20.060
possibly with a ticketing
system or even an audit

00:26:20.060 --> 00:26:22.490
and review process to make
sure that the escalation

00:26:22.490 --> 00:26:25.370
of privileges is appropriate,
and then also to repair

00:26:25.370 --> 00:26:27.750
the glass, to take away the
access when the incidents are

00:26:27.750 --> 00:26:28.250
resolved.

00:26:32.280 --> 00:26:34.030
Now that we've dealt
with roles let's deal

00:26:34.030 --> 00:26:37.120
with securely accessing
my cloud, my VMs.

00:26:37.120 --> 00:26:40.390
So securely accessing
data to and from my VMs

00:26:40.390 --> 00:26:42.585
involves three
categories of access.

00:26:42.585 --> 00:26:43.960
The first thing
I need to do is I

00:26:43.960 --> 00:26:46.390
need to be able to SSH
into and connect to my VMs

00:26:46.390 --> 00:26:48.430
and figure out what's
going on sometimes.

00:26:48.430 --> 00:26:51.743
Second, I need to be able to
get securely from my VMs--

00:26:51.743 --> 00:26:53.410
workloads in my VMs
need to securely get

00:26:53.410 --> 00:26:57.970
to Google APIs to access
storage and workflow

00:26:57.970 --> 00:27:01.670
engines and all the other
products that we offer.

00:27:01.670 --> 00:27:03.940
And finally, your VMs
will need to phone home

00:27:03.940 --> 00:27:08.020
and make calls to your on
prem services or to other VMs

00:27:08.020 --> 00:27:12.340
or to VMs running in other
clouds, and do so securely.

00:27:12.340 --> 00:27:15.390
I'm going to go through
each of those in turn here.

00:27:15.390 --> 00:27:16.600
Oh, wait.

00:27:16.600 --> 00:27:18.380
Did you feel that, Sarui?

00:27:18.380 --> 00:27:23.280
It was like a disturbance, as
if a million credentials were

00:27:23.280 --> 00:27:26.160
just created and
suddenly silenced

00:27:26.160 --> 00:27:29.280
because they were published to a
GitHub repository in the cloud.

00:27:32.660 --> 00:27:35.120
Breaks my heart.

00:27:35.120 --> 00:27:37.010
This is what we
want you to avoid.

00:27:37.010 --> 00:27:39.500
In particular,
unmanaged credentials

00:27:39.500 --> 00:27:42.140
is one of the greatest
security threats

00:27:42.140 --> 00:27:46.130
that we've seen in almost
all cloud activity.

00:27:46.130 --> 00:27:50.270
When we work with our
customers we strongly

00:27:50.270 --> 00:27:52.460
encourage you to use
fully managed credentials

00:27:52.460 --> 00:27:55.400
in every circumstance that you
can, where the platform takes

00:27:55.400 --> 00:27:59.900
care of rotating credentials,
keeping them in escrow

00:27:59.900 --> 00:28:03.290
and protecting those
credentials from loss or leakage

00:28:03.290 --> 00:28:07.890
or publishing to a
repository in the sky.

00:28:07.890 --> 00:28:11.810
And so do this, first, let's
look at the SSH'ing to my VM

00:28:11.810 --> 00:28:13.760
scenario.

00:28:13.760 --> 00:28:15.920
I just deployed a
10,000 VM cluster.

00:28:15.920 --> 00:28:21.590
It was doing something cool,
trust me, but five of those VMs

00:28:21.590 --> 00:28:23.412
stopped working and
I need my on-callers

00:28:23.412 --> 00:28:25.370
to be able to get into
those VMs and figure out

00:28:25.370 --> 00:28:26.660
what was going wrong.

00:28:26.660 --> 00:28:33.680
So some customers will
go and push some SSH keys

00:28:33.680 --> 00:28:37.010
around into those VMs, but
the downside of that approach

00:28:37.010 --> 00:28:39.440
is that when those
keys are lost or leaked

00:28:39.440 --> 00:28:42.650
or an employee who had access
to a key leaves your company,

00:28:42.650 --> 00:28:44.780
you're now stuck
with a key that's

00:28:44.780 --> 00:28:48.890
leaked that an employee
that left your company

00:28:48.890 --> 00:28:51.860
might still be able
to SSH into your VMs.

00:28:51.860 --> 00:28:54.410
Instead, we'd like to recommend
that our customers use

00:28:54.410 --> 00:28:55.760
what we call OS Login.

00:28:55.760 --> 00:28:57.830
It's a product in
the Compute system

00:28:57.830 --> 00:29:00.260
which you can enable by--
when you create your VMs,

00:29:00.260 --> 00:29:02.360
you can enable it
by setting metadata

00:29:02.360 --> 00:29:03.920
on your virtual machine.

00:29:03.920 --> 00:29:06.080
You enable OS Login
and enable OS Login

00:29:06.080 --> 00:29:08.060
two-factor authentication,
which requires

00:29:08.060 --> 00:29:10.610
that your engineers provide
a security key whenever

00:29:10.610 --> 00:29:12.980
they SSH into a VM.

00:29:12.980 --> 00:29:15.440
And then you can control
access to your virtual machines

00:29:15.440 --> 00:29:20.020
using IAM permissions, using the
OS Login, or the OS admin login

00:29:20.020 --> 00:29:20.870
roles.

00:29:20.870 --> 00:29:23.480
And the difference being
whether you let them

00:29:23.480 --> 00:29:26.935
log in as themselves
or as root users.

00:29:26.935 --> 00:29:28.310
And if you put
this all together,

00:29:28.310 --> 00:29:33.590
you have complete assurance
that all access to that VM

00:29:33.590 --> 00:29:35.270
is tied to the user lifecycle.

00:29:35.270 --> 00:29:37.250
When the user leaves your--

00:29:37.250 --> 00:29:39.380
when my [? on-caller ?]
leaves my company,

00:29:39.380 --> 00:29:44.030
for whatever reason, the access
to the key that they have

00:29:44.030 --> 00:29:45.600
doesn't matter anymore.

00:29:45.600 --> 00:29:47.600
They can take that key
and publish it to GitHub

00:29:47.600 --> 00:29:49.850
and it still doesn't give
them access to my VMs.

00:29:53.190 --> 00:29:56.910
Next, we want to talk about
authenticating my VM securely

00:29:56.910 --> 00:30:00.090
to my Google APIs and
my Google resources.

00:30:00.090 --> 00:30:03.150
To achieve this
task, what we do is

00:30:03.150 --> 00:30:06.030
what we call binding a service
account to a virtual machine.

00:30:06.030 --> 00:30:08.340
The first step is to
create a service identity

00:30:08.340 --> 00:30:09.960
in the Google Cloud console.

00:30:09.960 --> 00:30:13.110
The second step is to
grant that service identity

00:30:13.110 --> 00:30:15.840
access to all the things that I
think my workload needs access

00:30:15.840 --> 00:30:17.130
to.

00:30:17.130 --> 00:30:20.010
Third and finally, when I'm
creating the virtual machine

00:30:20.010 --> 00:30:22.560
I select which
service account I want

00:30:22.560 --> 00:30:24.870
that virtual machine to run as.

00:30:24.870 --> 00:30:28.950
Every access that's made with
client libraries running inside

00:30:28.950 --> 00:30:31.770
of that virtual
machine authenticates

00:30:31.770 --> 00:30:34.920
the cloud as the service
account identity.

00:30:34.920 --> 00:30:37.680
The long-term keys are held
in escrow and the only things

00:30:37.680 --> 00:30:40.380
that the workload in
the virtual machine

00:30:40.380 --> 00:30:42.780
gets is a short-term
access token, which can

00:30:42.780 --> 00:30:44.400
be used to apply to API calls.

00:30:46.910 --> 00:30:50.990
Now, one of the
common difficulties

00:30:50.990 --> 00:30:54.275
with using this feature is
something called access scopes.

00:30:54.275 --> 00:30:56.150
It's something that
you're required to select

00:30:56.150 --> 00:30:58.340
when using the feature.

00:30:58.340 --> 00:31:00.660
It is a great source of
confusion to customers,

00:31:00.660 --> 00:31:02.480
we find.

00:31:02.480 --> 00:31:07.160
The default is set to allow
default access, which isn't

00:31:07.160 --> 00:31:08.430
always the easiest to use.

00:31:08.430 --> 00:31:10.880
We recommend instead
using "allow full access

00:31:10.880 --> 00:31:15.320
to cloud APIs" and what we
call the Cloud Platform scope.

00:31:15.320 --> 00:31:19.370
This ensures that you have
easy access to call APIs.

00:31:19.370 --> 00:31:22.100
It's a tool to effectively
make client library

00:31:22.100 --> 00:31:23.420
integration simpler.

00:31:23.420 --> 00:31:25.347
And then use IAM
permissions and IAM roles

00:31:25.347 --> 00:31:27.680
granted to the service account
as your security feature.

00:31:30.610 --> 00:31:35.250
Now, talking about VMs
that need to phone home.

00:31:35.250 --> 00:31:37.000
A lot of times, you're
going to be running

00:31:37.000 --> 00:31:39.790
only part of your workload
on Compute and some of it's

00:31:39.790 --> 00:31:41.110
going to be running elsewhere.

00:31:43.930 --> 00:31:45.970
To address that
particular problem,

00:31:45.970 --> 00:31:49.030
we use the same approach
that we used for calling

00:31:49.030 --> 00:31:51.130
into Google APIs previously.

00:31:51.130 --> 00:31:53.830
You'd create a service account,
you attach it to the VM

00:31:53.830 --> 00:31:55.780
when you've constructed it.

00:31:55.780 --> 00:32:00.050
The code in the VM can then
get an OIDC Access Token,

00:32:00.050 --> 00:32:03.820
which is an industry standard
compliant OpenID Connect

00:32:03.820 --> 00:32:07.120
token that can be
verified by verifying

00:32:07.120 --> 00:32:11.980
the signature in the token with
the OIDC endpoint for Google.

00:32:11.980 --> 00:32:14.145
And the token contains
all the information

00:32:14.145 --> 00:32:15.520
that you could
ever possibly need

00:32:15.520 --> 00:32:17.685
to authorize access to this VM.

00:32:17.685 --> 00:32:19.060
It contains the
service identity,

00:32:19.060 --> 00:32:22.930
it contains the project ID, the
zone, the VM name, the creation

00:32:22.930 --> 00:32:24.730
date, everything you
possibly ever need.

00:32:27.820 --> 00:32:30.070
Again, it's
standards-compliant so it

00:32:30.070 --> 00:32:34.810
works with all open source
OIDC-compliant libraries.

00:32:34.810 --> 00:32:37.150
It gives you strong
proof of identity,

00:32:37.150 --> 00:32:39.310
and again, you don't have
to manage credentials.

00:32:39.310 --> 00:32:42.800
So everyone's safe.

00:32:42.800 --> 00:32:45.688
Next, I'd like to talk about
other kinds of threats.

00:32:45.688 --> 00:32:47.230
Other kinds of
organizational threats

00:32:47.230 --> 00:32:50.730
to your virtual machine fleet.

00:32:50.730 --> 00:32:53.910
In particular,
rootkits and bootkits.

00:32:53.910 --> 00:32:55.650
When you have people
who can SSH in

00:32:55.650 --> 00:32:57.990
or malicious software
that can hack

00:32:57.990 --> 00:33:00.540
its way into the
front door, you always

00:33:00.540 --> 00:33:03.270
have a risk that the software
that you're running in your VM

00:33:03.270 --> 00:33:04.770
fleet is not the
software you think

00:33:04.770 --> 00:33:09.260
it is unless you have a strategy
to make sure that it is.

00:33:09.260 --> 00:33:12.610
And the approach to solve
that-- what you can use

00:33:12.610 --> 00:33:16.020
is a product we call Shielded
VMs, which just launched to GA.

00:33:16.020 --> 00:33:18.600
It allows for a
trusted chain of boot

00:33:18.600 --> 00:33:22.310
from the virtual TPM of the
VM through the firmware,

00:33:22.310 --> 00:33:24.060
through the operating
system, to make sure

00:33:24.060 --> 00:33:26.700
that all the software running
in the operating system

00:33:26.700 --> 00:33:28.270
has been vetted and vouched for.

00:33:28.270 --> 00:33:31.110
If you have a rootkit that
installs random malware

00:33:31.110 --> 00:33:33.510
into a kernel module in
your operating system,

00:33:33.510 --> 00:33:36.000
we'll detect it and we'll
notify you and tell you

00:33:36.000 --> 00:33:37.800
which VMs have
been hacked so you

00:33:37.800 --> 00:33:41.780
can take remediative action.

00:33:41.780 --> 00:33:45.650
To use this feature, you
can go to the boot disk

00:33:45.650 --> 00:33:48.230
operating system
images on Compute

00:33:48.230 --> 00:33:53.120
and you can filter
by the Shielded VM

00:33:53.120 --> 00:33:56.155
provided operating
system images.

00:33:56.155 --> 00:33:57.530
And then when you
create your VM,

00:33:57.530 --> 00:33:59.920
you turn on the
Shielded VM option.

00:33:59.920 --> 00:34:02.190
And you can notice
that this is an option,

00:34:02.190 --> 00:34:05.930
and so you might worry, maybe
my developers cannot create

00:34:05.930 --> 00:34:06.570
a Shielded VM.

00:34:09.630 --> 00:34:12.080
And that's why we recommend
that you combine that

00:34:12.080 --> 00:34:15.949
with the trusted boot pattern
that we showed you before

00:34:15.949 --> 00:34:18.110
and organizational policies.

00:34:18.110 --> 00:34:24.949
You can apply the same pattern
that we showed you before,

00:34:24.949 --> 00:34:27.473
where you can have your
operating system images being

00:34:27.473 --> 00:34:29.515
vetted and curated by your
images team, published

00:34:29.515 --> 00:34:31.040
to your organization.

00:34:31.040 --> 00:34:33.130
And then you can apply
an organizational policy

00:34:33.130 --> 00:34:35.409
to the rest of your
organization to both require

00:34:35.409 --> 00:34:38.860
that all VMs be created with
the Shielded VM feature.

00:34:38.860 --> 00:34:41.620
And also, to ensure that
the only ones that are used

00:34:41.620 --> 00:34:45.150
are the ones that you've
vetted and approved.

00:34:45.150 --> 00:34:46.920
And again, to turn
that on you just

00:34:46.920 --> 00:34:51.030
go to the organization policy
control panel in your Cloud

00:34:51.030 --> 00:34:55.230
console and turn on Shielded
VMs and your trusted image

00:34:55.230 --> 00:34:57.809
projects settings.

00:34:57.809 --> 00:35:01.215
Should I go back?

00:35:01.215 --> 00:35:02.460
Got to take a picture.

00:35:02.460 --> 00:35:05.190
[INAUDIBLE] OK.

00:35:05.190 --> 00:35:08.670
Finally, I'd like to
talk about setting up

00:35:08.670 --> 00:35:11.370
secure-by-default
behaviors for your Cloud.

00:35:11.370 --> 00:35:13.890
Like I said before, we are
sometimes in a tough spot

00:35:13.890 --> 00:35:15.540
because we're trying
to sell a Cloud.

00:35:15.540 --> 00:35:18.510
And to sell a Cloud, we need
to make it easy to use and also

00:35:18.510 --> 00:35:20.040
secure at the same time.

00:35:20.040 --> 00:35:21.930
To achieve this,
oftentimes the defaults

00:35:21.930 --> 00:35:24.923
will come out of the box
with things easy to use.

00:35:24.923 --> 00:35:27.340
You'll get the owner permissions
when you create a project

00:35:27.340 --> 00:35:29.310
so you don't have to worry about
figuring out the permission

00:35:29.310 --> 00:35:30.930
model just to deploy a function.

00:35:33.440 --> 00:35:35.600
We do recommend that you
secure those projects

00:35:35.600 --> 00:35:37.558
after you created them,
especially when they're

00:35:37.558 --> 00:35:38.960
production resources.

00:35:38.960 --> 00:35:41.330
Now, to achieve
this, what we find

00:35:41.330 --> 00:35:43.840
is a factory pattern
works wonders.

00:35:43.840 --> 00:35:47.690
And every one of our customers
that cares about security

00:35:47.690 --> 00:35:51.650
seems to apply this mechanism
in one way or another.

00:35:51.650 --> 00:35:55.550
The basic idea is, again, to
set up a workflow using Jenkins

00:35:55.550 --> 00:35:57.560
or Terraform or Cloud
Function or whatever

00:35:57.560 --> 00:36:00.440
it is, where your
employees, your developers,

00:36:00.440 --> 00:36:04.520
can click on a button
hosted by you that

00:36:04.520 --> 00:36:07.130
creates the resource in GCP.

00:36:07.130 --> 00:36:10.100
And then sets up all the
configuration exactly

00:36:10.100 --> 00:36:12.450
as you need it.

00:36:12.450 --> 00:36:15.500
So for instance,
to achieve this,

00:36:15.500 --> 00:36:18.090
you can first go to your
organization policy, your IAM

00:36:18.090 --> 00:36:18.590
policy.

00:36:18.590 --> 00:36:20.240
There, you'll find
a policy grant

00:36:20.240 --> 00:36:22.220
that enables everyone
in your domain

00:36:22.220 --> 00:36:23.940
to be able to create a project.

00:36:23.940 --> 00:36:24.980
You can disable that.

00:36:24.980 --> 00:36:26.480
Take that permission away.

00:36:26.480 --> 00:36:29.210
Instead, you can grant that
permission to your workflow

00:36:29.210 --> 00:36:34.412
engines that are allowed to
create vetted, secure projects.

00:36:34.412 --> 00:36:36.120
Again, you can use
Jenkins and Terraform.

00:36:36.120 --> 00:36:38.500
Anything you want.

00:36:38.500 --> 00:36:41.850
And for us, some starting
points for thinking

00:36:41.850 --> 00:36:47.130
about how you'd like to
make your default projects--

00:36:47.130 --> 00:36:49.780
you could, for instance, delete
the default Compute service

00:36:49.780 --> 00:36:50.280
account.

00:36:50.280 --> 00:36:52.530
You can neuter its
permissions, which

00:36:52.530 --> 00:36:56.460
start out with fairly
broad editor permissions.

00:36:56.460 --> 00:36:59.400
You can delete the VPC network
that is created on the project

00:36:59.400 --> 00:37:03.090
and instead connect that
project to a secured shared VPC

00:37:03.090 --> 00:37:06.180
that you use for your
entire organization

00:37:06.180 --> 00:37:08.367
or for sections of
your organization.

00:37:08.367 --> 00:37:10.200
You can attach billing
accounts to make sure

00:37:10.200 --> 00:37:14.880
that all the costs centering
goes to the right billing

00:37:14.880 --> 00:37:15.600
account.

00:37:15.600 --> 00:37:17.508
You can set up audit
logs and billing exports

00:37:17.508 --> 00:37:19.800
to make sure that you have
full visibility as to what's

00:37:19.800 --> 00:37:22.860
going on in those projects
when you unleash it

00:37:22.860 --> 00:37:24.160
to your developers.

00:37:24.160 --> 00:37:26.160
And again, this gives you
centralized governance

00:37:26.160 --> 00:37:28.860
and control and guide
rails for your developers.

00:37:28.860 --> 00:37:31.530
And it eliminates
all the challenges

00:37:31.530 --> 00:37:34.080
of making your developers
get easy access--

00:37:34.080 --> 00:37:36.560
rapid access-- to a
production environment,

00:37:36.560 --> 00:37:39.000
or to develop their code,
or to create a production

00:37:39.000 --> 00:37:40.320
environment.

00:37:40.320 --> 00:37:42.830
And with that, I'll
hand it over to Sirui

00:37:42.830 --> 00:37:44.700
to tell you more about
keeping track of what

00:37:44.700 --> 00:37:46.470
your developers are doing.

00:37:46.470 --> 00:37:47.550
SIRUI SUN: Thanks, Cache.

00:37:47.550 --> 00:37:50.430
So, another type of question
we get from customers a lot

00:37:50.430 --> 00:37:54.330
is, how do I more easily start
to understand everything that's

00:37:54.330 --> 00:37:55.810
going on in my organization?

00:37:55.810 --> 00:37:58.800
And this is a key part of
centralized administration

00:37:58.800 --> 00:38:00.720
because in the
beginning, we talked

00:38:00.720 --> 00:38:02.490
about how you can
extend permissions

00:38:02.490 --> 00:38:05.320
to your organization,
to your org admin team,

00:38:05.320 --> 00:38:06.790
for example, to view everything.

00:38:06.790 --> 00:38:09.300
But that's not the
same thing as being

00:38:09.300 --> 00:38:12.780
able to look over that
content in an easy way

00:38:12.780 --> 00:38:15.060
to analyze what all is going on.

00:38:15.060 --> 00:38:17.670
And so I want to start by
talking about some of the tools

00:38:17.670 --> 00:38:21.630
that we offer to start giving
you that kind of 360 degree

00:38:21.630 --> 00:38:22.922
view of your organization.

00:38:22.922 --> 00:38:24.630
So, the first tool I
want to talk about--

00:38:24.630 --> 00:38:26.047
this has been
around for a while--

00:38:26.047 --> 00:38:27.405
are audit logs.

00:38:27.405 --> 00:38:30.030
And I don't have time to get too
deep into the nuances of audit

00:38:30.030 --> 00:38:32.610
logs, but I will talk
about a few types

00:38:32.610 --> 00:38:35.305
that are really helpful for
this particular use case.

00:38:35.305 --> 00:38:36.930
So, the first type
I want to talk about

00:38:36.930 --> 00:38:38.760
are admin activity logs.

00:38:38.760 --> 00:38:40.650
So, these logs are
generated whenever

00:38:40.650 --> 00:38:42.660
an administrative
action is taken

00:38:42.660 --> 00:38:46.290
on both the GCE resource but
also most other types of GCP

00:38:46.290 --> 00:38:47.380
resources.

00:38:47.380 --> 00:38:49.830
So for example, if I
had a user named Alice

00:38:49.830 --> 00:38:52.130
and she created a VM in
a particular project,

00:38:52.130 --> 00:38:55.500
we'll create an admin activity
log automatically for you.

00:38:55.500 --> 00:38:57.930
And that will include
what type of VM

00:38:57.930 --> 00:39:02.040
it was, what time it
happened, and other details

00:39:02.040 --> 00:39:04.260
that give you more
visibility into what exactly

00:39:04.260 --> 00:39:05.160
was happening.

00:39:05.160 --> 00:39:09.170
These audit logs are enabled
by default in all projects.

00:39:09.170 --> 00:39:11.200
In addition, they are
generated in real time.

00:39:11.200 --> 00:39:13.560
So they really give
you that up-to-date,

00:39:13.560 --> 00:39:16.800
up to the second details
about what all is going on

00:39:16.800 --> 00:39:19.505
in your organization.

00:39:19.505 --> 00:39:20.880
A second type of
audit log that's

00:39:20.880 --> 00:39:24.660
important to know at this point
is the data access audit log.

00:39:24.660 --> 00:39:29.155
So, these talk about data
reads and data accesses.

00:39:29.155 --> 00:39:31.530
These tend to be a little bit
more verbose so they're not

00:39:31.530 --> 00:39:33.390
turned on by
default. You actually

00:39:33.390 --> 00:39:35.400
have to go and turn
them on yourself

00:39:35.400 --> 00:39:37.333
but when you do so,
that information

00:39:37.333 --> 00:39:38.250
will start flowing in.

00:39:38.250 --> 00:39:39.990
And so in the use
cases where you're

00:39:39.990 --> 00:39:42.840
really interested in knowing
even about the reads,

00:39:42.840 --> 00:39:45.500
we highly suggest that
you turn these on.

00:39:45.500 --> 00:39:48.830
So, with audit logs you get kind
of this moment-by-moment set

00:39:48.830 --> 00:39:51.230
of events for your
entire organization.

00:39:51.230 --> 00:39:53.360
And we release these and
we go to our customers

00:39:53.360 --> 00:39:54.750
and they say,
well, this is good,

00:39:54.750 --> 00:39:58.670
but it's difficult sometimes
to understand the precise state

00:39:58.670 --> 00:40:01.400
of your world at a
particular moment in time

00:40:01.400 --> 00:40:03.500
if I just have a set
of events leading up

00:40:03.500 --> 00:40:05.810
to that particular
moment, right?

00:40:05.810 --> 00:40:08.120
And it's possible to
construct that from audit logs

00:40:08.120 --> 00:40:10.230
but it gets a little tricky.

00:40:10.230 --> 00:40:12.060
And so in response
to that feedback,

00:40:12.060 --> 00:40:15.380
we've actually recently
released a second tool

00:40:15.380 --> 00:40:17.600
that we call the
Cloud Asset Inventory.

00:40:17.600 --> 00:40:19.760
And this tool answers
the question of,

00:40:19.760 --> 00:40:23.240
at a particular moment in time,
what resources and policies

00:40:23.240 --> 00:40:25.895
were there in my organization,
in a particular folder,

00:40:25.895 --> 00:40:27.020
or in a particular project?

00:40:29.465 --> 00:40:31.340
This tool, this particular
API, will actually

00:40:31.340 --> 00:40:33.830
look back in the last five
weeks so it has knowledge

00:40:33.830 --> 00:40:35.190
of the last five weeks.

00:40:35.190 --> 00:40:37.040
And so you can ask a
question like, hey,

00:40:37.040 --> 00:40:40.520
last Saturday at 2:00 AM when
I had a security incident

00:40:40.520 --> 00:40:43.160
or something like that, what
were the VMs that were running

00:40:43.160 --> 00:40:44.300
in my organization?

00:40:44.300 --> 00:40:46.570
And what were the policies
applied on those VMs?

00:40:46.570 --> 00:40:49.490
And so you can start to do those
forensics-type investigations

00:40:49.490 --> 00:40:50.990
or you can start--
you could imagine

00:40:50.990 --> 00:40:53.330
you could use it for any
other number of use cases.

00:40:53.330 --> 00:40:56.360
And it's great for
printing out that state.

00:40:56.360 --> 00:41:00.020
Because it has knowledge of your
environment for the last five

00:41:00.020 --> 00:41:02.390
weeks, it can also
tell you about how

00:41:02.390 --> 00:41:05.690
a particular resource changed
over the past five weeks.

00:41:05.690 --> 00:41:07.383
So, kind of like a
scrubbing over time,

00:41:07.383 --> 00:41:09.050
you can see how a VM
might have evolved.

00:41:09.050 --> 00:41:12.260
You can see who gained or
lost access to that VM.

00:41:12.260 --> 00:41:15.080
And so you can think of these
as the two building blocks

00:41:15.080 --> 00:41:17.390
to start getting an
understanding of what's

00:41:17.390 --> 00:41:19.040
going on in your organization.

00:41:19.040 --> 00:41:21.320
I won't delve too much
deeper here, other

00:41:21.320 --> 00:41:23.900
than to say that we'll be
going much deeper into this

00:41:23.900 --> 00:41:26.960
at tomorrow's session called
Best Practices for GCE

00:41:26.960 --> 00:41:28.970
Enterprise Deployments.

00:41:28.970 --> 00:41:31.330
Now, having released
both of these tools, what

00:41:31.330 --> 00:41:33.020
we see a lot of times
is that customers

00:41:33.020 --> 00:41:36.380
will go through and build
very similar solutions on top

00:41:36.380 --> 00:41:37.580
of these building blocks.

00:41:37.580 --> 00:41:39.440
They'll build solutions
to go and list out

00:41:39.440 --> 00:41:41.930
all of the assets in
their organization

00:41:41.930 --> 00:41:44.570
and they might also scan
the events and the assets

00:41:44.570 --> 00:41:46.700
in their organization for
things that they don't

00:41:46.700 --> 00:41:47.840
want to have happen, right?

00:41:47.840 --> 00:41:50.900
Scan for vulnerabilities,
scan for that the right set

00:41:50.900 --> 00:41:52.748
of firewall rules are
set up, et cetera.

00:41:52.748 --> 00:41:54.290
And so in response
to that, we wanted

00:41:54.290 --> 00:41:57.240
to deliver some of that
functionality out of the box.

00:41:57.240 --> 00:42:01.440
And so we recently released to
beta the Cloud Security Command

00:42:01.440 --> 00:42:01.940
Center.

00:42:01.940 --> 00:42:04.520
This is going to provide a
lot of that functionality out

00:42:04.520 --> 00:42:05.180
of the box.

00:42:05.180 --> 00:42:07.300
It's going to be a single
pane of glass that's

00:42:07.300 --> 00:42:09.620
going to let you understand
what resources are

00:42:09.620 --> 00:42:12.110
in your organization, let you
explore what resources are

00:42:12.110 --> 00:42:15.560
in your organization, and also,
it'll proactively be scanning

00:42:15.560 --> 00:42:19.670
for vulnerabilities and
proactively informing you of,

00:42:19.670 --> 00:42:23.218
for example, if you have
suspicious traffic going on,

00:42:23.218 --> 00:42:25.760
if you have VMs with insecure
firewalls and things like that.

00:42:25.760 --> 00:42:28.970
So based on the feedback we
got from what type of solutions

00:42:28.970 --> 00:42:31.550
our customers built, based
on our building blocks,

00:42:31.550 --> 00:42:33.770
we've built some of that
out of the box for you.

00:42:33.770 --> 00:42:35.270
And if you want to
go further if you

00:42:35.270 --> 00:42:37.100
want to modify that
or change something

00:42:37.100 --> 00:42:38.770
based on your
security posture, you

00:42:38.770 --> 00:42:40.650
can always go back to
your building blocks

00:42:40.650 --> 00:42:41.900
and build that out from there.

00:42:44.700 --> 00:42:46.200
Last section.

00:42:46.200 --> 00:42:48.180
I want to talk a little
bit about the tools

00:42:48.180 --> 00:42:49.680
that we provide you
as our customers

00:42:49.680 --> 00:42:52.410
to ensure your
privacy and to ensure

00:42:52.410 --> 00:42:56.190
that you're in full control
over your data in Google Cloud.

00:42:56.190 --> 00:42:57.940
So one thing we hear
a lot from customers,

00:42:57.940 --> 00:43:00.180
especially those in more
tightly regulated industries

00:43:00.180 --> 00:43:03.490
like banking, is that they want
full control over their data

00:43:03.490 --> 00:43:04.740
that they upload to the cloud.

00:43:04.740 --> 00:43:06.190
It might be very sensitive.

00:43:06.190 --> 00:43:09.270
They might be beholden to
regulatory needs to do so.

00:43:09.270 --> 00:43:13.230
And one way that this translates
is, most of the times customers

00:43:13.230 --> 00:43:16.170
ask for more control over
how their data is encrypted

00:43:16.170 --> 00:43:18.510
in the cloud so that they
can manage that encryption

00:43:18.510 --> 00:43:20.427
and so that they could,
for example, take away

00:43:20.427 --> 00:43:24.600
the encryption keys protecting
their data so that no one has

00:43:24.600 --> 00:43:25.800
access to that data anymore.

00:43:25.800 --> 00:43:27.353
Not even us, Google.

00:43:27.353 --> 00:43:28.770
So, the first thing
I want to talk

00:43:28.770 --> 00:43:30.270
I want to say
before I proceed is

00:43:30.270 --> 00:43:34.060
that by default all
data in Google Cloud

00:43:34.060 --> 00:43:37.030
is encrypted at rest
regardless of configuration.

00:43:37.030 --> 00:43:40.410
So even if you go in
and you don't configure

00:43:40.410 --> 00:43:42.510
any of this data, any
of this encryption,

00:43:42.510 --> 00:43:44.280
we'll still do it for
you automatically.

00:43:44.280 --> 00:43:46.892
But we do give you the
tools to go further.

00:43:46.892 --> 00:43:48.600
A best practice that's
quickly developing

00:43:48.600 --> 00:43:51.780
is that customers can use
another managed service

00:43:51.780 --> 00:43:54.210
that Google Cloud provides
called Google Key Management

00:43:54.210 --> 00:43:55.000
Service.

00:43:55.000 --> 00:43:58.380
This is a managed service that
allows you to manage keys.

00:43:58.380 --> 00:44:01.630
So, you can create keys, rotate
keys, delete them at any time.

00:44:01.630 --> 00:44:03.060
And then you can
apply these keys

00:44:03.060 --> 00:44:05.760
to your disks, to your
images and snapshots,

00:44:05.760 --> 00:44:08.480
and any other content
that you store in GCE.

00:44:08.480 --> 00:44:10.230
And we'll start using
that key that you've

00:44:10.230 --> 00:44:13.910
managed to go ahead and
start encrypting your data.

00:44:13.910 --> 00:44:16.530
I should also note that
this whole flow is supported

00:44:16.530 --> 00:44:19.110
with-- it's integrated with
IAM and audit logging, so

00:44:19.110 --> 00:44:20.912
all the tools that
we just talked about.

00:44:20.912 --> 00:44:22.620
So you can see who's
doing the encryption

00:44:22.620 --> 00:44:24.210
and where these keys are.

00:44:24.210 --> 00:44:27.270
And Cloud KMS has
a broad support

00:44:27.270 --> 00:44:28.980
for all of the set
of security standards

00:44:28.980 --> 00:44:32.880
that we've been hearing are
required from our customers.

00:44:32.880 --> 00:44:33.990
So, what this looks like.

00:44:33.990 --> 00:44:36.140
You would start by creating
a key and a key ring

00:44:36.140 --> 00:44:36.920
in Cloud KMS.

00:44:36.920 --> 00:44:38.490
That's a very quick task.

00:44:38.490 --> 00:44:40.370
It should just take
you a few minutes.

00:44:40.370 --> 00:44:44.900
And you can specify what the
rotation period of this key,

00:44:44.900 --> 00:44:47.930
what encryption standard
it uses, et cetera.

00:44:47.930 --> 00:44:50.480
And then when you go and
create a GCE disk or image

00:44:50.480 --> 00:44:54.890
or snapshot, you will just
point GCE to that particular key

00:44:54.890 --> 00:44:56.330
that you've just created.

00:44:56.330 --> 00:44:59.660
And just like that, we'll
start using that particular key

00:44:59.660 --> 00:45:00.990
to protect your data.

00:45:00.990 --> 00:45:03.710
And so what that means
is as-needed later

00:45:03.710 --> 00:45:06.750
you can go revoke or
disable access to that key.

00:45:06.750 --> 00:45:08.660
This is what it looks
like both in the UI

00:45:08.660 --> 00:45:11.960
and in the G Cloud
command line tool.

00:45:11.960 --> 00:45:14.570
And from there, once you've
disabled access what you'll

00:45:14.570 --> 00:45:17.500
find is that GCE will no longer
have access to that information

00:45:17.500 --> 00:45:18.000
at all.

00:45:18.000 --> 00:45:21.380
So if you try to attach a disk
that's protected by the revoked

00:45:21.380 --> 00:45:24.140
key to a VM you'll see
that that action fails

00:45:24.140 --> 00:45:27.650
and that will fail until you
restore access to the key.

00:45:27.650 --> 00:45:29.540
And so in this way
you have full control

00:45:29.540 --> 00:45:32.810
over who has access to
your data on Google Cloud.

00:45:35.480 --> 00:45:38.150
Another question that we get
from customers is, well hey,

00:45:38.150 --> 00:45:39.770
you're Google, how do I
know that you're not using

00:45:39.770 --> 00:45:41.078
my data for other means, right?

00:45:41.078 --> 00:45:43.370
How do I know you're not
using it to train some-- yeah,

00:45:43.370 --> 00:45:46.410
you know, to advertise
or something like that.

00:45:46.410 --> 00:45:48.620
Now, here I want to say
again that at Google Cloud,

00:45:48.620 --> 00:45:51.140
we do not access your
data for any other reason

00:45:51.140 --> 00:45:54.440
than those necessary to fulfill
our contractual obligations

00:45:54.440 --> 00:45:55.410
to you.

00:45:55.410 --> 00:45:58.550
And in fact, when we
looked at the data

00:45:58.550 --> 00:46:02.150
internally, over 99% of when
we do these data accesses,

00:46:02.150 --> 00:46:03.860
they're to fulfill
support requests.

00:46:03.860 --> 00:46:05.360
So it's where
customers are actually

00:46:05.360 --> 00:46:08.370
asking us to do these
to do these actions.

00:46:08.370 --> 00:46:10.650
But you don't have to
take our word for it.

00:46:10.650 --> 00:46:13.220
We have a feature called
Access Transparency logs.

00:46:13.220 --> 00:46:16.460
They're another type of audit
logs that we just talked about.

00:46:16.460 --> 00:46:18.470
And what they do
is they'll tell you

00:46:18.470 --> 00:46:22.130
in near-real-time and
without cost any time

00:46:22.130 --> 00:46:24.920
Google accesses your
data for any reason.

00:46:24.920 --> 00:46:26.010
All right.

00:46:26.010 --> 00:46:27.620
The one caveat
here is that these

00:46:27.620 --> 00:46:32.780
are available for only a certain
subset of our support packages.

00:46:32.780 --> 00:46:35.330
And you can find out
more details online.

00:46:35.330 --> 00:46:37.130
But they're very easy to enable.

00:46:37.130 --> 00:46:39.860
So as long as you have the right
permissions, that's step one--

00:46:39.860 --> 00:46:42.170
we have a particular role
for this Access Transparency

00:46:42.170 --> 00:46:43.080
admin--

00:46:43.080 --> 00:46:45.140
you can just go into a
particular project folder

00:46:45.140 --> 00:46:46.230
organization.

00:46:46.230 --> 00:46:50.030
And you'll find a little
toggle in IAM admin settings.

00:46:50.030 --> 00:46:51.860
And from there you
can go and you'll just

00:46:51.860 --> 00:46:54.487
see that however you're
getting your logs,

00:46:54.487 --> 00:46:56.570
maybe it's through the
Stackdriver Logging Portal,

00:46:56.570 --> 00:46:59.482
you'll start seeing these
Access Transparency logs.

00:46:59.482 --> 00:47:00.690
So, what does that look like?

00:47:00.690 --> 00:47:03.290
Here's an example of an
Access Transparency log.

00:47:03.290 --> 00:47:05.060
It will be generated
once for every action

00:47:05.060 --> 00:47:06.985
that we take on your data.

00:47:06.985 --> 00:47:08.360
And it will tell
you-- in yellow,

00:47:08.360 --> 00:47:09.860
you can see it tells
you the reason,

00:47:09.860 --> 00:47:11.660
so why did we access your data?

00:47:11.660 --> 00:47:13.160
Here in particular,
we're doing this

00:47:13.160 --> 00:47:15.410
because of a customer-initiated
support request.

00:47:15.410 --> 00:47:17.580
And we'll attach the
case number for you.

00:47:17.580 --> 00:47:20.090
And then in green you can
see what exactly Google did.

00:47:20.090 --> 00:47:24.320
So you can see the resource
that we were operating on,

00:47:24.320 --> 00:47:27.730
the API calls that we
made when we did this,

00:47:27.730 --> 00:47:30.998
and a number of other
details relevant to the case.

00:47:30.998 --> 00:47:33.540
And so, you don't have to take
our word for what I just said.

00:47:33.540 --> 00:47:38.110
You can actually go in
and verify yourselves.

00:47:38.110 --> 00:47:41.610
And so with that, that brings
us to the end of our talk.

00:47:41.610 --> 00:47:43.720
We started by laying
down some principles,

00:47:43.720 --> 00:47:45.428
and hopefully we've
been able to build up

00:47:45.428 --> 00:47:48.197
to a set of best practices
to go apply those principles.

00:47:48.197 --> 00:47:49.030
Thank you very much.

00:47:49.030 --> 00:47:52.380
[APPLAUSE]

